## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP on strings, palindrome precomputation, and reconstruction. It also requires handling a non-trivial scoring function beyond simple lengths.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome table isPal[i][j] for substring s[i..j]
- dist[i][j] stores number of distinct characters in s[i..j]
- DP state dp[i][t]: best score for first i chars partitioned into t palindromes
- Transition: for all j < i with isPal[j][i-1], dp[i][t] = max(dp[i][t], dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1])
- Reconstruction via parent pointers to recover cut positions
- Time/space: O(n^2 k) time and O(n^2 k) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalPartitionScore(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) { isPal[i][i] = true; dist[i][i] = 1; }
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (len === 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
      const seen = new Set();
      for (let p = i; p <= j; p++) seen.add(s[p]);
      dist[i][j] = seen.size;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (isPal[j][i-1] && dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
          const cand = dp[j][t-1] + dist[j][i-1];
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    cuts.push([j, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return { score: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory with rolling DP when n is large?
- How does the approach adapt if the score uses a different function, e.g., length of palindrome minus distinct count?
- What are the practical limits for n and k for this to run within time constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
New DP angle focusing on subarray range gain with exact k partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP partitioning
- Precomputing range max/min
- Reconstruction through pointers
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxGainKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const gain = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let l=0;l<n;l++){
    let mn = A[l], mx = A[l];
    for(let r=l;r<n;r++){
      mn = Math.min(mn, A[r]);
      mx = Math.max(mx, A[r]);
      gain[l][r] = mx - mn;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      take[i][t] = take[i-1][t];
      if(t>0){
        for(let l=1;l<=i;l++){
          const val = dp[l-1][t-1] + gain[l-1][i-1];
          if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = l-1; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=k;
  while(t>0){
    const lminus1 = take[i][t];
    const l = lminus1 + 1;
    res.unshift([l, i]);
    i = lminus1; t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][k], blocks: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct blocks?
- How would the solution change if subarrays must have at least a fixed length L?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a non-adjacent selection constraint and exact count, forcing clarity on state definition and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on two dimensions (i, t) with take/skip choices. 
- Non-adjacency enforced by using i-2 when taking i. 
- Backtracking recovers chosen indices; space optimization possible.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonAdjacent(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      const val = (i>=2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t===1 ? 0 : -Infinity)) + A[i-1];
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = true; }
    }
  }
  let res = [], i=n, t=k;
  while(i>0 && t>0){
    if(take[i][t]){ res.push(i); i-=2; t--; } else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], indices: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most k selections? 
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a classic DP over partitioning with a non-linear segment cost (max). It checks understanding of states, transitions, and reconstruction, plus edge cases like k=1 and k=n.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Segment cost as max over A[j..i-1] and dynamic maintenance of that max
- Reconstruction of endpoints via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minSumMaxPartitions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let mx = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
        mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
        if (dp[j][t - 1] !== INF) {
          dp[i][t] = Math.min(dp[i][t], dp[j][t - 1] + mx);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact endpoints efficiently?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) with additional structure or pruning for typical inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on subsequences with transition costs; requires deriving a 2D DP with O(n^2 m) time and backpointers. Edge cases: m=1 yields 0. Consider tie-breaking on reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on subsequences; 2D states; O(n^2 m) time; backtracking via parents; rolling arrays for space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubseq(A, m){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=>Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){
    for(let t=2; t<=m; t++){
      for(let p=0; p<i; p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[p]);
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, end = -1;
  for(let i=0; i<n; i++){ if(dp[i][m] < best){ best = dp[i][m]; end = i; } }
  const idx = []; let t=m, cur = end;
  while(t >= 1){ idx.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  idx.reverse();
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes DP with non-overlapping blocks and a non-additive subarray score (bitwise OR). It requires a state that handles gaps and a compact representation of OR values, plus a reconstruction strategy.

## Key Concepts
- DP over k blocks with a required gap
- Distinct ORs per end i optimization (≤ O(log MAXA))
- Backtracking pointers for block endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs and practical constants

## Code Example
```javascript
// DP sketch
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  const ors = orStates[i]; // [{val,left}, ...]
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const {val,left} of ors) {
      const prev = (left > 1) ? dp[left-2][t-1] : 0;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], prev + val);
      parent[i][t] = { left, val };
    }
