## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

